  "description": "<p>From GitHub Issue <a href=\"https://github.com/fcrepo/fcrepo/issues/2269\" title=\"smart-link\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://github.com/fcrepo/fcrepo/issues/2269</a> </p>\n\n<div class=\"preformatted panel\" style=\"border-width: 1px;\"><div class=\"preformattedContent panelContent\">\n<pre>➜  ~ curl --head -s http://fedora.local.lan/rest/ | grep Content\nAccept-External-Content-Handling: copy,redirect,proxy\nContent-Type: text/turtle;charset=utf-8\nContent-Length: 0\n➜  ~ \n</pre>\n</div></div>\n\n<p>According to the <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#name-content-length\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><ins>HTTP spec</ins></a>:</p>\n\n<blockquote><p>A server MAY send a Content-Length header field in a response to a HEAD request (<a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#HEAD\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><ins>Section 9.3.2</ins></a>); a server MUST NOT send Content-Length in such a response unless its field value equals the decimal number of octets that would have been sent in the content of a response if the same request had used the GET method.</p></blockquote>",
